course: dictionary
lesson: 8397
title: A ruffian is a rough troublemaker
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A ruffian is a rough, violent or lawless person, often a man or a boy who causes trouble. You will meet this old-fashioned word in stories and in history books, where highwaymen, pirates and street gangs are called ruffians.
explain: Look at the clues around the word. In «The incorrigible rogue and hardened ruffian», the judge is calling Toad a bad lot. Incorrigible means can't be made better, so the judge thinks Toad is beyond help.
explain: In The Wind in the Willows, the stoats and weasels who take over Toad Hall are called ruffians. The words «take over» tell you they are rough and do not follow the rules. Test your meaning by swapping it into the sentence and checking that it still makes sense.
fact: A ruffian is a rough, violent or lawless person, especially a man or a boy who causes trouble.
fact: Ruffian is a noun, and its plural is ruffians.
fact: «Incorrigible» means can't be made better.
fact: Ruffian is an old-fashioned word, so you will mostly find it in stories and history.
fact: Today grandparents sometimes use «ruffian» half-jokingly for a noisy boy, which means they are not meaning it too seriously.
exercise: What is a ruffian? | *A rough, lawless person who causes trouble | A type of old coat | A small river bird | A kind person who helps lost animals
exercise: In The Wind in the Willows, who are called ruffians? | Mole and Ratty, who are kind friends | *Stoats and weasels who take over Toad Hall | The horses pulling the carts
exercise: Grandad calls a noisy boy a ruffian, half-jokingly. What does half-jokingly mean? | *Partly as a joke, not meant too seriously | Very angrily, with no joke at all | Only in a court of law | In a whisper, so nobody hears
test: In «The incorrigible rogue and hardened ruffian», which word means can't be made better? | ruffian | hardened | *incorrigible | rogue
test: Which sentence uses ruffian in its real meaning? | The ruffian was a tall tree in the park | The ruffian of the class brought his cake to school | *The ruffians broke the shop window and ran off | The ruffian sang a happy song at the party
exam: Which best describes a ruffian in an old story? | *A rough, lawless person who causes trouble | A kind doctor who helps sick animals | A gentle, shy neighbour who bakes bread | A musical instrument played in a band
